00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.390
Because no matter what we always
want more computational power.

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:23.280
We have, as scientists and
engineers, we have the ability

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:25.980
to fill the available
computational power with no

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:31.110
problem. And I think that could
go on to infinity. And then

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.360
finally, the uncertainty
quantification tools are

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.600
maturing. And I don't mean to
claim this as a mature field.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.660
But it's, you know, all the good
works been done. But it's gotten

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:42.600
to the point where it's actually
practicable, you can actually

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:49.950
start implementing UQ practices
in real problems. And so just

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:55.500
the low res showing one example,
a very complex model. So in red,

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:57.690
I'm going to give the
counterpart to each of those

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:01.140
opportunities, sort of the
challenge that it presents, we

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:05.070
can develop many more new
materials, but it creates an

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:08.160
enormously high dimensional
design space. So if you think

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:12.660
about an ATA that has, say, five
element components, there's an

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.330
enormous number of possible
combinations of those. And

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:20.490
certainly the full continuum of
all the proportions of those

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:24.630
high throughput characterization
and testing is terrific, it

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:27.330
creates large quantities of data
that we have to deal with, we

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:30.240
have to deal with data flows,
and typically that data is low

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:33.660
fidelity. So you're balancing
sort of high throughput, low

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:39.030
fidelity data with a little bit
low fidelity, or high fidelity,

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:43.020
but very sparse data. And so
there's a tricky balance there.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:49.950
True multi scaling, real models
of these systems are often

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:53.700
computationally infeasible, even
with a lot of computational

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:58.920
power. So we need clever ways to
attack that. And finally, UQ

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:03.960
adds cost. We're finding some
very good tools, but but it is

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:10.140
costly. So I can't address all
of those in this talk. But I do

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.870
want to think about the issue,
the slow models do create a

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:17.070
bottleneck for design decisions.
So if I'm thinking about our aim

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:24.570
D facility, if the laser shock
system is sort of asking, is it

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:29.700
worth testing this particular
specimen, and I send it to a

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.330
model that takes 24 hours to
answer, then I'm getting no

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:38.250
value from the fact that laser
shot can happen in minutes. And

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:41.700
so I don't want my models to be
so slow that any decision that's

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.430
made is waiting on some long,

00:12:46.049 --> 00:12:51.809
slow running model, and need
have fast decisions. So I think

00:12:51.809 --> 00:12:56.489
today, I'll focus on these two
particular pieces. One is, how

00:12:56.489 --> 00:13:00.239
can we enhance the data we have?
So for example, can we enhance

00:13:00.239 --> 00:13:05.369
high for low fidelity data to
create high fidelity data? And

00:13:05.369 --> 00:13:11.729
how can we address multi scaling
in a way that's efficient? So

00:13:11.729 --> 00:13:17.549
I'll start with the first one is
kind of a talk in two parts. So

00:13:17.699 --> 00:13:22.229
here's our problem statement. If
we're given limited, three

00:13:22.229 --> 00:13:27.779
dimensional, or even only two
dimensional data, could we

00:13:27.779 --> 00:13:31.979
reconstruct a three dimensional
microstructure? Or better yet an

00:13:31.979 --> 00:13:37.229
ensemble of those? So often, in
particularly in a high

00:13:37.229 --> 00:13:39.809
throughput setting, you're not
given three dimensional

00:13:39.809 --> 00:13:43.289
information? Is there a way for
us to take the two dimensional

00:13:43.289 --> 00:13:47.279
information and generate micro
structures that have similar

00:13:47.309 --> 00:13:51.449
characteristics? So again, our
goal is to generate an ensemble

00:13:51.449 --> 00:13:55.439
of micro structures that match
what are key characteristics of

00:13:55.439 --> 00:13:58.439
the microstructure? They're not
the same microstructure, a

00:13:58.439 --> 00:14:02.669
different instantiation of the
same kind of microstructure. And

00:14:02.669 --> 00:14:05.999
one challenge we face is that
traditional stochastic

00:14:05.999 --> 00:14:11.249
simulation does not work well.
So there are a couple of

00:14:11.249 --> 00:14:16.409
challenges here, say a binary
material like this is very non

00:14:16.409 --> 00:14:20.489
Gaussian and its distribution,
right? It's basically two Dirac

00:14:20.489 --> 00:14:24.719
delta functions, which is maybe
the extreme of non Gaussian

00:14:24.719 --> 00:14:28.559
unity. So things like
translation processes to

00:14:28.559 --> 00:14:32.729
generate microstructures won't
work, or at least won't work

00:14:32.729 --> 00:14:37.349
very well. The other issue is
that these kinds of irregular

00:14:37.349 --> 00:14:40.319
microstructures aren't as simple
as maybe a model where you'd say

00:14:40.319 --> 00:14:43.109
I'm going to put, you know,
circular inclusions down in

00:14:43.109 --> 00:14:48.479
space randomly. You can't use
that here. So what are some

00:14:48.479 --> 00:14:54.119
descriptor descriptors that we
might want to think about? One

00:14:54.119 --> 00:14:57.749
could be something like the two
point probability function,

00:14:58.109 --> 00:15:03.239
which basically asks if I Put a
vector down randomly in the

00:15:03.239 --> 00:15:06.989
space, what's the probability
that its two endpoints lie in

00:15:06.989 --> 00:15:11.009
poor space. And so on the right
upper right, you can see

00:15:12.959 --> 00:15:16.499
basically, a typical probability
function, its maximum is at

00:15:16.499 --> 00:15:20.099
delta x delta y both equaling
zero, meaning you're comparing

00:15:20.099 --> 00:15:23.459
the point to itself. So it makes
sense, those are appointed very

00:15:23.459 --> 00:15:26.969
well correlated to itself. And
that sort of correlation dies

00:15:26.969 --> 00:15:30.629
off as you go further away from
the the vector gets longer and

00:15:30.629 --> 00:15:35.399
longer. Another characteristic
you might think about is a pore

00:15:35.399 --> 00:15:38.069
size distribution, which
basically says if you pick a

00:15:38.069 --> 00:15:42.329
random point in the pore space,
what is the distance to the

00:15:42.329 --> 00:15:48.059
closest solid space. And a
typical function looks something

00:15:48.059 --> 00:15:53.639
like what I'm showing here kind
of starts at a peak at zero and

00:15:53.699 --> 00:16:01.049
dies out as you go to longer and
longer distances. Now, a problem

00:16:01.049 --> 00:16:05.639
here is this. Two point
probabilities don't often

00:16:05.639 --> 00:16:08.939
capture all the characteristics.
So these are some results from

00:16:09.149 --> 00:16:12.329
actually a fairly old paper,
where a student and I were

00:16:12.329 --> 00:16:15.779
working on a simulated annealing
approach to simulate these micro

00:16:15.779 --> 00:16:18.209
structures. And we wanted to
match the two points,

00:16:18.209 --> 00:16:23.579
statistics. So the upper row is
the original image. The second

00:16:23.579 --> 00:16:27.089
row is a simulated image. And
the two points statistics are

00:16:27.089 --> 00:16:30.089
the same between the first and
the second row. And what you see

00:16:30.089 --> 00:16:34.199
is it's okay for the circular
inclusion microstructure on the

00:16:34.199 --> 00:16:36.989
left the first one. But if you
look at the other three, there's

00:16:36.989 --> 00:16:42.809
a lot of information lost shapes
conductivity, you see glimmers

00:16:42.809 --> 00:16:44.999
of what the original
microstructure was, but it's

00:16:44.999 --> 00:16:49.289
really not the same at all. And
we kind of hit a point where we

00:16:49.289 --> 00:16:52.799
said, Well, we tried using three
point correlations, and then

00:16:52.799 --> 00:16:56.099
improve the problem improve the
simulation somewhat, but it was

00:16:56.099 --> 00:17:02.129
incredibly slow to do that. And
in the end, it came down to the

00:17:02.159 --> 00:17:05.729
things that we see as people who
say, Well, this is more

00:17:05.729 --> 00:17:08.519
squiggly, or that's more
connected. But that's not so

00:17:08.549 --> 00:17:13.799
easy to quantify. So the what is
missing question kind of hung

00:17:13.799 --> 00:17:18.539
out there, until we started
looking at some of the tools out

00:17:18.539 --> 00:17:22.919
there related to machine
learning. So the idea here is to

00:17:22.919 --> 00:17:28.499
use transfer learning. The VGG
19 network is a network that's

00:17:28.529 --> 00:17:32.819
commonly used for recognizing
objects. So is this a picture of

00:17:33.419 --> 00:17:40.979
a cat or a truck or an airplane.
And it performs that recognition

00:17:40.979 --> 00:17:44.729
through a series of finding just
all sorts of features, I don't

00:17:44.729 --> 00:17:47.549
even know there's no way to
describe them physically. But it

00:17:47.549 --> 00:17:51.479
just works with that image and
identifies all the relevant

00:17:51.479 --> 00:17:54.299
features that would make it
categorize into being a certain

00:17:54.299 --> 00:17:57.329
object. So you can think of the
same thing with microstructure

00:17:57.329 --> 00:18:01.919
we input a microstructure image.
And the VGG 19 model gives us a

00:18:01.919 --> 00:18:07.169
sense of the feature maps, a
bunch of aspects that we don't

00:18:07.169 --> 00:18:08.849
necessarily know how to
describe, but they're

00:18:08.849 --> 00:18:15.779
quantifiable. And so how we can
use those networks is let's say

00:18:15.779 --> 00:18:19.679
we have a target image, that we
will need to create another

00:18:19.679 --> 00:18:23.039
image with the same
characteristics. So we can take

00:18:23.039 --> 00:18:26.669
the target image and run it
through the VGG 19 model. And we

00:18:26.669 --> 00:18:32.579
can do the same thing with a
simulated image. And we can just

00:18:32.579 --> 00:18:37.979
start with white noise, and
compare the grim basis Graham

00:18:37.979 --> 00:18:40.859
gram matrix for each of them.
And the loss is the difference

00:18:40.859 --> 00:18:44.849
between those. So that last
tells you how far away is my

00:18:45.269 --> 00:18:49.649
sort of initial simulation from
the target. And there are other

00:18:49.649 --> 00:18:52.829
things that we can add to this
loss function like total

00:18:52.829 --> 00:18:58.169
variation, which is basically
penalizing individual pixels,

00:18:58.439 --> 00:19:02.369
like noise. So it's a way of
sort of reducing noise in the

00:19:02.369 --> 00:19:04.919
image, but you can put whatever
you want into this loss

00:19:04.919 --> 00:19:13.289
function. combine it together to
identify total loss, different

00:19:13.289 --> 00:19:15.899
again, essentially difference
between the target and where you

00:19:15.899 --> 00:19:19.679
are with your current image, and
asked Have I minimize, have I?

00:19:20.159 --> 00:19:26.789
is the loss less than some
threshold? If it is, well,

00:19:26.789 --> 00:19:29.669
great, we're done. We have a
reconstructed microstructure

00:19:29.669 --> 00:19:33.269
that has a lot of the statistics
or the characteristics of the

00:19:33.269 --> 00:19:38.009
original. If not, then I need to
update and basically the

00:19:38.009 --> 00:19:42.209
algorithm uses a gradient based
optimization, sort of an atom

00:19:42.629 --> 00:19:47.009
optimizer to update that image
and then we do the comparison

00:19:47.009 --> 00:19:49.619
again, and we just keep going
and iterating and changing that

00:19:49.619 --> 00:19:55.559
image until it matches our
target image in some reasonable

00:19:55.559 --> 00:20:00.689
way. This actually was drawn
from some work by A way Chen's

00:20:00.689 --> 00:20:03.749
group at Northwestern where they
just looked at the gram matrix

00:20:03.749 --> 00:20:07.049
loss, and we've been playing
with what goes into the loss

00:20:07.049 --> 00:20:09.089
function, in addition to that.

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:17.430
Alright, so I'm gonna do
something there.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:23.130
So this optimization based
reconstruction, we match the

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:27.570
statistical descriptors. And the
VGG 19 feature maps from the

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:30.180
target. Another thing that we
added to the loss function was

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:33.180
the two point probability. So we
wanted to actually minimize the

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.940
difference in the two point
probability between the original

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:39.660
and the reconstructed. And on
the right, you can see sort of a

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:46.260
target. On the left is a quick
GIF of an image going from white

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:50.880
noise converging to a simulated
microstructure. So you can see

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:53.970
what happens is it iterates
through this process from

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:58.410
basically right now for fuzzy
white noise through to a final

00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:04.020
image. And our final image, we
get a very good match and things

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:06.780
like the two point probability
function and the pore size

00:21:06.780 --> 00:21:09.570
distribution function. So those
basic statistics look really

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:14.310
good. Now, that was just a
single image. And other thing we

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:20.880
thought about was, we have a
sample that it's actually a CT

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:25.710
scan of a three dimensional
porous ceramic, and it has 180.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:29.130
It's one of its dimensions at
185. So we took each of those

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:34.740
slices, and treated it as one
sample from the material. So I

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.860
think the way to think about
this is, you have a material,

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:40.740
that's all, you know, some bulk
of material, it's all the same

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:43.710
material. But if you looked at
the microstructure in one

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:46.380
region, and in another region,
you will get different micro

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:51.270
structures. And so we wanted to
capture that variability. So we

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:55.470
went slice by slice. And you can
see that there's a variation in

00:21:55.470 --> 00:22:00.270
porosity, and correlation
length, and in mean pore size

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:03.510
across all of these and again,
this is the experimentally

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:07.650
obtained data, the slices. Our
question was, do the

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:11.280
reconstructed microstructures
capture this. And so what we did

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:15.390
was for each slice, target
slice, we generated a simulated

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:20.070
slice. Again, you can see the
target and the simulated are not

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:23.940
the same thing, but they're
statistically similar. And we

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:28.680
went did that over the full 185
slices. And here, we're

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:33.330
comparing the Ricans
distribution of porosity, coral

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:36.660
length, correlation, length and
mean pore size in the

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:42.030
reconstructed in green, and the
target in blue. And if you look

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:45.420
at the means and the variances
below each plot, you can see

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:52.170
that we're essentially matching
definitely the mean, with almost

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:56.520
essentially no error and the
variance very well. And each of

00:22:56.520 --> 00:23:01.290
these distributions, and the
distributions match over each

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:04.890
other quite well. Now, again,
there's a statistical sampling.

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:09.360
That happens here, that would
mean that we wouldn't expect the

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:12.090
distributions to be exactly on
top of each other, because these

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:19.410
are different instantiations of
similar statistics. So we match

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:24.570
those statistics. But is that
really sufficient? And that was

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:28.440
a question that we asked
ourselves. And we thought about

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:32.370
the idea that that reason, you
would typically want to generate

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:35.130
these virtual microstructures
like this is because you're

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:39.360
using them to model something.
So you're, you would typically

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:42.720
simulate a microstructure, put
it through some sort of finite

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:47.070
element model to develop some
sort of prediction. And so we

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:49.740
thought, probably a good way to
test this is to look at some

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:53.400
average properties that would be
predicted, based on finite

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:56.580
element models of the
microstructure. So what we did

00:23:56.580 --> 00:24:00.960
is for every microstructure that
we simulated, we put it into a

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:06.000
finite element model, with
different properties for the

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.130
black and the white phase. And I
can't remember exactly, I

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:11.640
remember that the black phase
was typically high values, and

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:14.820
the white phase was low, we
didn't do full on pours, but she

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:18.270
doesn't think was like a low
stiffness or low conductivity.

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:25.110
And so then we predict the
stress distribution, the heat

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:28.740
flux distribution, and the
discharge distribution

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:37.590
associated with each of those
models. And overlaid that with

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.560
predictions from finite element
models of the original

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:45.270
microstructures. And again, you
see there's an excellent match

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:49.560
in the mean and the variance of
these quantities. Thermal

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:54.150
conductivity probably struggles
the most, and maybe if I go back

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:58.050
to that result for a second, you
can see the thermal conductivity

00:24:58.050 --> 00:25:01.920
in the middle. It's very low.
localized sort of paths of heat

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:05.370
flux. So we would expect that
that would be a little more

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:08.250
difficult to predict than, for
example, the Youngs modulus,

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:12.930
which is like an average sort of
quantity. So that shows up here,

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:15.990
but even with thermal
conductivity, there's a really

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:20.190
strong match between the
simulated and the actual

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:21.960
microstructure statistics.

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:31.710
So, what have we accomplished
here? If you compare this to

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:35.610
simulated annealing, which was
sort of the more traditional

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:39.750
approach, the transfer learning
here was orders of magnitude

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:43.170
less time, and achieve
significantly superior results.

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:45.480
So at some point, we just ran
out of patience with the

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:48.690
simulated annealing and cut it
off before it converged all the

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:57.300
way. But this was, we're talking
hours versus minutes. And the

00:25:57.330 --> 00:26:00.150
difference here, so you know, a
couple orders of magnitude

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:04.440
faster, to get a very good multi
loss based reconstruction on the

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:10.290
left. And we also were able to
apply this to a variety of

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:13.320
multi-phase materials, thinking
of polycrystal and kinds of

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:15.660
materials and get different
kinds of micro structures. If

00:26:15.660 --> 00:26:19.050
you think about look at the top
and the bottom, they're both

00:26:19.050 --> 00:26:22.050
poly crystalline, but they have
sort of different kinds of

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:28.890
features, and we're able to
capture that. So that's 2d. It's

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:31.500
interesting from the perspective
of being able to generate an

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:39.720
ensemble of, of microstructures.
But really, I think, what's more

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:42.840
exciting is the idea of being
able to go to full three

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:47.040
dimensional microstructure
simulation. So we go back to the

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:50.010
3d reconstruction. So this is
our basic image. This is again,

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:53.700
that porous ceramic, we've been
working with the images around

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:59.160
10 to the eighth, eighth voxels.
If we were to try to deal with

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:03.540
this full 3d structure and
optimize it, we'd be updating

00:27:03.540 --> 00:27:06.540
about 10, Meg's of data at every
optimization loop would be

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:12.420
pretty much memory intensive.
And furthermore, the VGG 19. And

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:16.560
similar models aren't really yet
trained for 3d images. So

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:19.620
instead, what we're doing is
working with 2d slices of this

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:24.840
image. And that's much more
easily parallelized. So I'll

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:30.270
show you some of the results
here. So here's the idea. Think

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:34.920
about this 3d structure, you
start with white noise, that's

00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:38.190
our starting condition. So every
pixel is randomly assigned black

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:43.680
or white, independent of the
pixels around it. And first, we

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:48.390
start thinking in the xy
direction, and we take each of

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:52.530
those white noise images, we run
it through our VGG 19 model, the

00:27:52.530 --> 00:27:59.310
Transfer model. And we update it
so that it turns into what's

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:04.020
viable as a microstructure in
the 2d slices. Now, of course,

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:08.010
one slice is independent of the
next. So you have like, within

00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:11.460
the slice, it looks good. But
across the slices, it won't look

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:15.570
as good. But so right now we
have, at this point, a deck,

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:19.740
think of a deck of cards, each
card has a reasonable

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:23.370
microstructure. But it's not
necessarily correlated to the

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:28.650
microstructure through the
cards. So you would end up with

00:28:28.650 --> 00:28:31.620
something like this, actually,
it'd be even worse in the first

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:34.170
step, because they'd be totally
uncorrelated, but you have this

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:37.230
sort of banded pneus, that
happens, right? Because what you

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:40.980
did to one slice was independent
of what you did to the previous

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:45.660
slice and slice after it. Um, so
this interlayer noise occurs.

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:50.760
But it does converge over
iterations, if we do the

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:55.170
following. If we take, say, the
slices with a light blue, and

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:59.610
now that's our deck of cards in
that direction. And we take each

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:03.240
of those sort of banded noisy
images, we make that the initial

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:07.890
condition for another transfer
model, transfer learning model,

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:14.550
we generate microstructures in
that direction. And then we go

00:29:14.550 --> 00:29:17.040
to the dark blue direction,
because now that's a little bit

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:20.940
banded in strange looking, and
we do the same thing, we go

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:24.870
through each slice. So that's
kind of highlighted here. This

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:28.740
is going in the x z direction,
we basically update the

00:29:28.740 --> 00:29:32.790
microstructure in that x, z
direction. And then we update

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:37.110
the slices in the y, z
direction. So in the process of

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:41.880
doing this, what happened in the
xy direction, now we've kind of

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:44.550
messed up what was originally a
pretty good looking set of

00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:50.010
microstructures within each
plane. So what we'll do is now

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:54.150
go pass through again, go in the
x, y direction, in the z

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:58.170
direction in the y, z direction,
maybe do three full passes, five

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:02.190
full passes. But what ends up
happening is, as you move

00:30:02.190 --> 00:30:06.210
forward, you're having to change
fewer and fewer voxels. So

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:08.700
you're starting to see
convergence in all three

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:17.100
directions, that ultimately
plays out into a good 3d model

00:30:17.970 --> 00:30:23.100
based on the 2d image. So again,
this is starting from a single

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:27.150
2d image and saying, if I assume
that the statistics are the same

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:32.790
in all the directions, I can go
ahead and generate a 3d

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:37.560
microstructure based on those
images. I could also have three

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:41.730
images, one in one in the xy
direction, one that's collected

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:44.700
in the y, z direction, and one
that's collected in the z

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:48.900
direction, if it's an an
isotropic material. And I could

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:53.010
follow the same process and
generate an an isotropic 3d

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:55.830
microstructure. So I'm not
limited to just isotropic. But

00:30:55.830 --> 00:31:02.070
that's what I showed here. Okay,
so the statistics of these 3d

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:06.930
microstructures look pretty
good. They get better and better

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:10.530
as we iterate more through the
model, this is after three

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:13.320
passes, if we went through five
passes, they might even get

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:20.280
closer. So by three passes, I
mean that we went direction 1233

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:24.630
times, so sort of nine passes
through in different directions.

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:29.910
And the pore size distribution
also looks very good in this 3d

00:31:29.970 --> 00:31:36.930
structure. We also use this to
look at polycrystalline

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:40.860
microstructure. So we have, we
only had the 2d slice that you

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:46.260
see above the upper left. And
from that, we generated a three

00:31:46.260 --> 00:31:50.730
dimensional volume. That's
statistically, it has the same

00:31:50.730 --> 00:31:55.350
characteristics as the 2d slice.
And actually, if you look at the

00:31:55.350 --> 00:32:01.230
grain size distribution from A
to D cut outs of the 3d volume,

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:05.880
they match almost imperceptibly
that almost exactly was the

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:13.980
target. So these efficient
simulations, why do we want

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:18.780
them, there are a few different
reasons. One is, the experiments

00:32:18.780 --> 00:32:22.770
are typically limited in the
amount that can be observed.

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:28.860
Right, so maybe you have a
system that can only look at 128

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:33.750
by 128 pixels or so you're
limited in the volume of

00:32:33.750 --> 00:32:35.910
material that you can
characterize at a given

00:32:35.910 --> 00:32:39.600
resolution. But what this tool
allows you to do is to actually

00:32:39.600 --> 00:32:44.280
generate then similar
microstructures, that are

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:48.630
larger, you could go smaller to
but I think larger is probably

00:32:48.630 --> 00:32:52.980
more motivating. So you're not
as limited in a model based on

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:55.560
the experimental observations,
you're not so limited by what

00:32:55.560 --> 00:33:01.950
the experiment is able to
resolve. Another piece that this

00:33:01.950 --> 00:33:06.030
enables is looking at the
uncertainty that's induced by

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:08.820
microstructure. So you can
generate an ensemble of micro

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:13.080
structures, and therefore get an
ensemble of responses. So you

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:16.530
can actually use that to feed a
model that would create your

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:20.730
would generate some sort of
uncertainty quantification. And

00:33:20.730 --> 00:33:23.820
then the third reason, or the
third value of this is the

00:33:23.820 --> 00:33:29.850
ability to go from a 2d image to
a set of similar 3d

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:34.770
microstructures. So if you have
a 2d reading of even in

00:33:34.770 --> 00:33:37.770
different directions of what a
particular material looks like,

00:33:38.160 --> 00:33:43.080
you can come up with a
reasonable representation of

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:51.360
what the 3d microstructure might
be. So that's my pitch for one

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:56.820
piece of trying to enhance data
that's serving a data driven

00:33:56.820 --> 00:33:59.580
system like this. Yes.

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:06.750
This is a good time to ask about
the first part. Yeah, sure. I

